      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 611,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   **Two hydrogen atoms**\n*   **One oxygen atom**\n\nThese atoms are bonded together tightly. Water is also famous for being the **\"Universal Solvent,\"** which means it can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This ability is crucial for chemical reactions in nature and inside living organisms.\n\n### 2. The Physical View\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** What we commonly think of as water (rivers, oceans, rain).\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). *Uniquely, solid water (ice) is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats.*\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F) or evaporates into the air.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Geological View\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). It is constantly moving in a cycle known as the **water cycle** (or hydrologic cycle):\n1.  **Evaporation:** The sun heats up water, turning it into vapor that rises into the atmosphere.\n2.  **Condensation:** The vapor cools and turns back into liquid drops, forming clouds.\n3.  **Precipitation:** Water falls back to Earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail.\n4.  **Collection:** Water gathers in rivers, lakes, and oceans, ready to start the cycle again.\n\n### 4. The Biological View\nWater is the foundation of all known life. \n*   The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**.\n*   Cells—the building blocks of life—are mostly water; it provides the watery environment (cytoplasm) necessary for cellular processes to happen.\n*   It regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, and flushes out waste.\n*   Plants need water to perform photosynthesis, the process by which they create food and release oxygen.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule made of hydrogen and oxygen, but it is paradoxically the most complex and vital substance on our planet. Without it, life as we know it could not exist."
